3.1 Environment Setup (GitHub Codespaces)
Because Codespaces provides a pre-configured Docker runtime, Minikube runs seamlessly using the Docker driver:

# 1. Install kubectl CLI
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
rm kubectl

# 2. Install Minikube
curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube
rm minikube-linux-amd64

# 3. Start cluster with the Docker driver
minikube start --driver=docker

# 4. Verify node health
kubectl get nodes


3.2 Declarative Workload: Deployments & Self-Healing
Naked Pods (kubectl run) lack self-healing. Production deployments rely on Deployments, which control underlying ReplicaSets to guarantee application availability.

Save as nginx-deployment.yaml:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app
  labels:
    tier: frontend
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "64Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "128Mi"

Apply and verify:


kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml
kubectl get deployments
kubectl get replicasets
kubectl get pods -l app=web-app


Self-Healing Test:

# Delete one Pod manually:
kubectl delete pod $(kubectl get pods -l app=web-app -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")

# Immediately list pods:
kubectl get pods -l app=web-app

Observation: The Deployment Controller immediately detects that current replicas (1) do not match desired replicas (2), scheduling an immediate replacement[cite: 2].

3.3 Decoupling Configuration: ConfigMaps & Secrets
To satisfy 12-Factor app architecture, configuration must be decoupled from container images.

Save as app-config.yaml:


apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: web-config
data:
  APP_ENV: "production"
  FEATURE_FLAG: "enabled"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: web-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:
  DB_PASSWORD: "SuperSecretPassword123"


Apply the configuration:

kubectl apply -f app-config.yaml

Update nginx-deployment.yaml to inject the configuration:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app
  labels:
    tier: frontend
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: web-config
        - secretRef:
            name: web-secret
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "64Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "128Mi"

Apply and verify variable injection:

kubectl apply -f nginx-deployment.yaml

POD_NAME=$(kubectl get pods -l app=web-app --field-selector=status.phase=Running -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")
kubectl exec $POD_NAME -- env | grep -E "APP_ENV|FEATURE_FLAG|DB_PASSWORD"


3.4 Service Discovery, Endpoints, & EndpointSlices
Because Pod IP addresses are ephemeral, a Service provides a durable DNS name and virtual IP (ClusterIP).

Save as web-service.yaml:


apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: web-app
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80


Apply and inspect the discovered IP mappings:

Bash

kubectl apply -f web-service.yaml

# Verify Service creation
kubectl get svc web-service

# Check EndpointSlice (modern replacement for Endpoints)
kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web-service

Access the service locally from Codespaces:

kubectl port-forward service/web-service 8080:80

(Codespaces prompts an "Open in Browser" notification under the Ports view to confirm HTTP 200 from Nginx).

